  <iati-identifier>XI-IATI-EC_FPI-2022-ACT-61403</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XI-IATI-EC_FPI" secondary-reporter="0" type="15">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">European Commission - Service for Foreign Policy Instruments</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Supporting resilience  to  increased  hybrid threats in the Western Balkans</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This 18-month action will help mitigate disinformation and cyber-security risks in the Western Balkans relating to the Russian war of aggression against Ukraine and other factors of polarisation.In a first component, the action will support efforts to increase information resilience in Bosnia and Herzegovina, Kosovo*,[1]Montenegro and Serbia. Activities under this component will focus on strengthening independent media and fact-checking networks including their outreach and analytical capability. This component will also support legislators and institutions responsible for media sector regulation in their efforts to strengthen information resilience and awareness, as well as assist the integration of ethical considerations in private-sector funded media- and advertisement-activities. A second component will further strengthen cyber security with EU partners with an elevated hybrid threat level, notably Albania, North Macedonia and Montenegro with emphasis on enhancing incidence response capacities. This action is in line with the Brdo EU-Western Balkans Summit Declaration of 2021, which foresees increased cooperation on disinformation and cyber security. It further complements and paves the way for ongoing and future EU-financed activities under under IPA III cooperation.</narrative>
  </description>
